    "question": {
      ":question/id": "d59a7bd0-7d50-5c65-bf27-b748e75aad8a",
      ":question/problem": "Find the domain of the function $f(x,y)=\\dfrac{\\sqrt{y-x^2}}{\\sqrt{2-\\left(x^2+y^2\\right)}}$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The numerator radicand may equal zero. The denominator radicand must be strictly positive, since its square root is the complete denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-x^2&\\ge 0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad y-x^2\\ge0\\\\\n2-\\left(x^2+y^2\\right)&>0\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad x^2+y^2<2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n\nTherefore, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\,:\\,y-x^2\\ge0,\\ x^2+y^2<2\\}.\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/requires-calculator": false,
